# Draft registration for the ADR 0005 proposal. A Decision vertex is created
# only after human acceptance and merge, never while this plan remains draft.
"@context": "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/context/v1"
"@id": "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/plan-batched-deletion-primitive"
"@type": "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/ontology/Plan"
label: "Plan: propose typed S3 multi-object deletion"
intent: >
  Carry ADR 0005 through adversarial review and human acceptance, then — only
  under a separately authorized kernel-extension batch — deliver an additive
  AtomicKeyspace primitive for 1,000-key S3 DeleteObjects transport with honest
  per-key partial outcomes and unchanged single-key atomicity law.
acceptanceCriteria:
  - "A human explicitly accepts and merges ADR 0005; before that acceptance, no Decision vertex or accepted-log entry represents it as closed law."
  - "The accepted API uses sequential chunks of at most 1,000 unique keys and returns exactly one ordered deleted-or-typed-failure outcome per admitted key."
  - "A loopback partial-batch cut constructs mixed Deleted/Error entries in one request and the API reports exactly which keys were confirmed and which failed."
  - "BeforeEffect and AfterEffect whole-response cuts are constructible; both report the current chunk Unconfirmed and the untouched tail NotAttempted without fabricated success."
  - "Unconditional batch deletion writes no tombstone, bumps no incarnation counter, and touches no trim certificate, maintenance fence, or v3 chunk."
  - "The change is additive: published delete_many, DeleteOutcome, delete, delete_if_match, destroy, and their contracts remain unchanged; no batch is a transaction or conditional delete."
  - "The ADR and implementation ledger assigns A36-A45 without collision, all claims pass in CI, and a retained real-S3 run witnesses the assured provider path before publication."
  - "The primitive deletes only caller-supplied keys; it neither treats LIST as inventory authority nor claims durable consumer checkpointing or namespace completeness."
consumes:
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/invariant-kernel-extension"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/invariant-13-state"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/invariant-08-dependency-boundary"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/structure-kernel"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/decision-0001-atomic-keyspace"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/vertex/decision-0004-streaming-value-io-v2"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/path/docs/decisions/0005-batched-deletion.md"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/path/crates/yeetz-s3-kernel"
  - "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/path/crates/yeetz-sdk-s3"
tasks:
  - "Keep ADR 0005 explicitly proposed and register it only as this draft plan."
  - "Run the independent adversarial review and amend the proposal forward where required."
  - "Obtain human acceptance and merge; only then register ADR 0005 as a Decision and add it to the accepted structure log."
  - "Obtain separate human authorization for one firewalled kernel implementation batch."
  - "Implement the SDK one-chunk wire seam and additive AtomicKeyspace API without migrating existing methods."
  - "Implement both loopback fault cuts, prove A36-A45, and retain current-tip ci-dev plus real-S3 witnesses."
  - "Publish the next free 0.4.x closure release; orchestrate every downstream adoption separately."
witnesses:
  - "https://github.com/cleverunicornz/yeetz-s3-kernel/actions/runs/32658427460"
reflectDepth: 2
disposition:
  state: draft
  residual: >
    This draft does not assure human acceptance, implementation authorization,
    A36-A45 behavior, live-S3 support, publication, inventory completeness,
    durable consumer checkpoints, or downstream adoption. The shipped boundary
    remains the existing delete/delete_many/delete_if_match contracts.
source: "https://yeetz.dev/bedrock/path/docs/decisions/0005-batched-deletion.md"
body: |
  ## Proposal depth

  The complete semantics, Rust API sketches, alternatives, release plan, and
  A36-A45 oracle are in `docs/decisions/0005-batched-deletion.md`. This vertex
  routes that proposal through the execution graph; it does not close the fork
  and does not authorize implementation.

  ## Authorization gates

  Human acceptance and merge come first. The acceptance-time change writes the
  Decision vertex and extends the accepted ADR structure log. A second explicit
  human authorization is then required before the kernel implementation batch.
  Downstream yeetz epoch adoption remains a separate orchestration after the
  assured crate release.

  ## Reflection depth

  Depth 2 means reflection reads both this plan/ADR and the independent review,
  then checks the implementation witnesses and declared residuals rather than
  treating a green design-branch gate as proof that the primitive exists.
